{
  "dom_id": "step-q334761",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($\u03c4$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(\u0394x)=6(\u0394x)^{3}+4(\u0394x)^{4}$\n- $\u03c4(\u0394x)=0.5(\u0394x)^{4}+3(\u0394x)^{7}$\n- $e(\u0394x)=2(\u0394x)^{2}+(\u0394x)^{5}$",
  "worked_solution": "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(\u0394x)=O((\u0394x)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,\u0394x\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\u03c4(\u0394x)=O((\u0394x)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,\u0394x\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let\u2019s check each option:\n\n- For $e(\u0394x)=6(\u0394x)^{3}+4(\u0394x)^{4},$ the dominant term as $\u0394x\\to 0$ is $6(\u0394x)^{3}=6(\u0394x)^{3}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $\u03c4(\u0394x)=0.5(\u0394x)^{4}+3(\u0394x)^{7},$ the dominant term as $\u0394x\\to 0$ is $0.5(\u0394x)^{4}=0.5(\u0394x)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $e(\u0394x)=2(\u0394x)^{2}+(\u0394x)^{5},$ the dominant term as $\u0394x\\to 0$ is $2(\u0394x)^{2}=2(\u0394x)^{2}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
